.\" Manpage for erebus.
.\" Render with: man ./doc/erebus.1   (or `make man`)
.TH EREBUS 1 "2026-06-03" "erebus" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
erebus \- rootless, user-space VPN proxy for OpenVPN
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B erebus
.B \-\-config
.I file
.br
.B erebus
.B \-c
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B erebus
is an experimental VPN client that runs entirely in user space. Instead of
creating a kernel
.BR tun (4)/ tap (4)
interface, changing system routes, or requiring root, it speaks the OpenVPN
wire protocol itself, builds and parses IP packets in user space, maintains a
minimal TCP/IP stack, and exposes access to the VPN through ordinary local
sockets.
.PP
It is a VPN
.I agent
rather than a system-wide VPN: nothing about the host's networking is touched.
Connectivity is explicit and opt-in. Two directions are supported, either or
both at once:
.TP
.B Outbound
A local HTTP proxy (\fB[proxy-out]\fR) forwards requests from local clients to
resources reachable on the VPN.
.TP
.B Inbound
Port forwards (\fB[proxy-in]\fR) expose selected local services to VPN peers.
.PP
.B erebus
currently implements OpenVPN
.B static-key
mode (also called pre-shared-key, or wire-format v1) only, over either UDP or
TCP transport. TLS mode, certificates and the control channel are not yet
implemented (see
.B LIMITATIONS
below).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIfile\fR
Path to the configuration file. Required. May also be supplied through the
.B EREBUS_CONFIG
environment variable. With no configuration, usage is printed and
.B erebus
exits.
.PP
There are no other command-line options: everything else is driven by the
configuration file. This keeps invocation reproducible and credentials out of
the process list.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is INI-style: \fB[section]\fR headers, \fBkey = value\fR
pairs, and \fB;\fR or \fB#\fR comments. Which sections are present determines
what
.B erebus
does. A connection is started when an \fB[openvpn-server]\fR or an
\fB[ipsec-server]\fR section is found (exactly one should be present); the proxy
is started only if \fB[proxy-out]\fR is present; inbound forwards are started for
each entry under \fB[proxy-in]\fR.
.SS [erebus]
General client settings.
.TP
.B client-ip
The IPv4 address this client occupies on the VPN (e.g. \fB10.8.0.2\fR). Used in
OpenVPN static-key mode only, where addressing is fixed by configuration on both
ends rather than pushed by the server, so this must match the server's
expectation. It is also the address VPN peers use to reach any \fB[proxy-in]\fR
forwards. In \fB[ipsec-server]\fR mode this setting is ignored: the server
assigns the VPN address during the IKEv2 handshake.
.SS [openvpn-server]
The VPN server to connect to. Its presence triggers a connection.
.TP
.B proto
Transport: \fBudp\fR or \fBtcp\fR. \fBudp\fR is recommended; \fBtcp\fR is for
environments that block UDP and incurs the well-known TCP-over-TCP meltdown
risk under loss.
.TP
.B host
Server hostname or IP address.
.TP
.B port
Server port (OpenVPN's default is \fB1194\fR).
.TP
.B secret
Path to the OpenVPN static key, as generated by
.RB \(lq "openvpn \-\-genkey \-\-secret static.key" \(rq
and shared with the server out of band. A key direction may be appended
(\fB/path/to/static.key 0\fR or \fB... 1\fR); see
.B key-direction
below.
.TP
.B key-direction
Optional key direction, \fB0\fR or \fB1\fR, as an alternative to appending it to
.BR secret .
If unset on both sides, the same key material is used for sending and
receiving (bidirectional). For split send/receive keys one side must use
\fB0\fR and the other \fB1\fR. This mirrors OpenVPN's
.B key-direction
semantics exactly.
.TP
.B cipher
Data-channel cipher, case-sensitive. Supported:
.B AES-128-CBC, AES-192-CBC, AES-256-CBC, ARIA-128-CBC, ARIA-192-CBC,
.B ARIA-256-CBC, CAMELLIA-128-CBC, CAMELLIA-192-CBC, CAMELLIA-256-CBC.
Only CBC modes are implemented. AEAD modes (GCM) and the newer data-channel
formats are not.
.TP
.B auth
HMAC digest used to authenticate packets, case-sensitive. Supported, in the
order
.RB \(lq "openvpn \-\-show\-digests" \(rq
lists them:
.B MD5, SHA1, RIPEMD160, MD4, SHA256, SHA384, SHA512, SHA224, whirlpool,
.B BLAKE2s256, SHA3-224, SHA3-256, SHA3-384, SHA3-512, SHAKE128, SHAKE256, SM3.
The
.B cipher
and
.B auth
values must match the server's.
.SS [ipsec-server]
An IKEv2/IPsec server (interoperates with strongSwan) to connect to, as an
alternative to \fB[openvpn-server]\fR. Authentication is pre-shared key (PSK)
only. The negotiated crypto suite is fixed and must be enabled on the server:
IKE \fBaes256-sha256-modp2048\fR, ESP \fBaes256-sha256\fR (no PFS).
.IP
Because
.B erebus
is rootless and cannot send raw ESP (IP protocol 50), all traffic is
UDP-encapsulated (NAT-T);
.B erebus
forces this, so the server floats to port 4500. The client's VPN address is
.I not
configured \(em it is assigned by the server during the handshake via an IKEv2
configuration payload.
.TP
.B host
Server hostname or IP address.
.TP
.B port
The IKE port (UDP). Default \fB500\fR. Only set this if the server or an
intervening NAT remaps it.
.TP
.B natt-port
The NAT-T / ESP port (UDP). Default \fB4500\fR. As with
.BR port ,
only set it if it is remapped.
.TP
.B psk
The pre-shared key, matching the server's secret.
.TP
.B local-id
The identity (IDi) presented to the server. Any string the server's PSK
configuration accepts; a responder configured with \fB%any\fR accepts any value.
Default \fBerebus\fR.
.SS [proxy-out]
The local outbound HTTP proxy. Optional; if omitted, no proxy is started and
.B erebus
runs inbound-only (or as a bare connection that simply keeps the tunnel up and
answers pings).
.TP
.B address
Local address to listen on. Default \fB127.0.0.1\fR.
.TP
.B port
Local port to listen on. Default \fB11023\fR.
.PP
Point an HTTP client at it as an ordinary proxy, e.g.
.RB \(lq "http_proxy=http://127.0.0.1:11023 curl http://10.8.0.1/" \(rq.
The proxy resolves hostnames, opens a TCP connection across the VPN, forwards
the request, and relays the response (honouring Content-Length, chunked, and
close-delimited framing) back to the local client.
.SS [proxy-in]
Inbound port forwards. Optional. Each key is an arbitrary label; the value is:
.RS
.PP
.I label
.B =
.I vpn-port
.I local-host\fB:\fIlocal-port
.RE
.PP
For example
.RB \(lq "web = 8080 127.0.0.1:8080" \(rq
lets a VPN peer reach the local service on \fB127.0.0.1:8080\fR by connecting to
.I client-ip\fB:8080\fR
over the VPN. Each inbound connection is accepted by the user-space TCP stack
(passive open) and relayed to the local service. Several entries may be listed,
one per exposed port.
.SH EXAMPLES
A minimal outbound-only proxy over UDP:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[erebus]
client-ip = 10.8.0.2

[openvpn-server]
proto  = udp
host   = vpn.example.com
port   = 1194
secret = /etc/erebus/static.key
cipher = AES-256-CBC
auth   = SHA256

[proxy-out]
address = 127.0.0.1
port    = 11023
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run it and use it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ erebus --config /etc/erebus/erebus.ini &
$ http_proxy=http://127.0.0.1:11023 curl http://10.8.0.1/
.RE
.fi
.PP
Expose a local web server to VPN peers as well (add to the same file):
.PP
.nf
.RS
[proxy-in]
web = 8080 127.0.0.1:8080
.RE
.fi
.PP
A VPN peer then reaches it at
.RB \(lq "http://10.8.0.2:8080/" \(rq
(assuming \fBclient-ip = 10.8.0.2\fR).
.PP
The same outbound proxy, but against an IKEv2/IPsec (strongSwan) server with a
pre-shared key. Note there is no
.B client-ip
\(em the server assigns one:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[ipsec-server]
host = vpn.example.com
psk  = the-shared-secret

[proxy-out]
address = 127.0.0.1
port    = 11023
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I erebus.ini
The configuration file (any path; named with
.BR \-\-config ).
A documented template ships as
.B default-config.ini
in the source tree.
.TP
.I static.key
The OpenVPN static key shared with the server, referenced by
.BR secret .
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B erebus
deliberately favours correctness, clarity and portability over completeness and
raw throughput. Known constraints, all intentional at this stage:
.TP
.B Static-key OpenVPN only
No TLS control channel, no certificates, no
.BR PUSH_REPLY .
Addressing,
.BR cipher " and " auth
are configured statically and must match the server.
.TP
.B CBC ciphers only
No AEAD/GCM, no compression framing beyond the no-compress byte.
.TP
.B HTTP/1.x proxy with HTTPS CONNECT
The outbound proxy speaks plain HTTP and supports HTTPS
.B CONNECT
tunnelling for opaque TLS passthrough. No SOCKS or system-wide routing.
TLS-to-backend is not proxied.
.TP
.B Minimal TCP stack
The embedded TCP/IP stack supports the connection handshake, orderly teardown,
and reads/writes spanning multiple segments. It is stop-and-wait (one segment
sent per acknowledgement, every inbound segment acknowledged), with one packet
queue per connection and no concurrent duplex on a single connection. There is
no congestion control, window scaling, fast retransmit, RTO tuning, or
selective ACK. This bounds bulk throughput; see
.BR doc/stress-results.md .
.TP
.B IPv4 / TCP / ICMP-echo only
No IPv6. ICMP support is limited to answering echo requests so the client is a
good citizen on the VPN.
.TP
.B Inbound relay is single-threaded per connection
By design, to avoid racing on a connection's single packet queue; simplicity
over throughput. Access control is limited to exposure being opt-in via
.BR [proxy-in] .
.PP
See
.B ROADMAP.md
in the source tree for what later phases intend to add (OpenVPN TLS, IPsec/IKEv2,
performance work).
